 	$\ell(u,v) = \ell(u',v)$.
 \end{proposition}
 \begin{proof}
+	\leanok
 	\uses{def:line}
 	Fix $u' \in \ell(u,v)$, so $u' = u + tv$ for some $t \in \F_q$. Any
 	$a \in \ell(u,v)$ has the form $a = u + sv = u' + (s - t)v$, so
 	$\ell(u,v) \subseteq \ell(u',v)$. The symmetric argument gives the reverse
 	inclusion.
 \end{proof}

